<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/organic-chemistry-hydrocarbons">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Chemistry lesson. Three foundational lessons are prerequisites:
    <strong>atomic structure</strong> (why carbon bonds four times), <strong>the periodic
    table</strong> (where carbon sits among its neighbours, and why silicon falls short) and
    <strong>the mole and stoichiometry</strong> (molecular and empirical formulae are
    counting tools you will use constantly). <strong>States of matter</strong> explains why
    chain length moves boiling points.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain carbon's three-pillar monopoly: tetravalence, catenation, strong bonds to other elements;</li>
    <li>write molecular, displayed, empirical and skeletal formulae, and name simple compounds under IUPAC rules;</li>
    <li>predict alkane combustion products, complete and incomplete, and describe halogen substitution;</li>
    <li>outline fractional distillation and cracking, and use the bromine water test for unsaturation;</li>
    <li>explain why benzene's bonds are all identical, and how isomers differ in properties.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Organic chemistry is carbon's story. Carbon bonds to itself endlessly — chains, branches,
    rings — creating more possible compounds than every other element combined (tens of
    millions catalogued, and counting). The simplest families are the <strong>hydrocarbons</strong>:
    carbon and hydrogen only. Everything from natural gas to candle wax to the aromatics in
    paint thinner is one of them, differing by chain length and by how the carbons connect.
    This chapter builds the vocabulary of the whole organic tier — formulae, functional
    groups, naming — then walks the hydrocarbon families in order of growing unsaturation:
    alkanes, alkenes, aromatics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one structural change, one property shift — longer chains mean heavier, higher-boiling liquids" widget="plotter" config={boilCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> trace the curve across the chain-length axis and pair
    points with physical states from the paragraph below — short chains as gases, octane a
    volatile liquid, twenty-carbon chains waxy solids. Each chain length sits at its own
    point on the boiling axis, and that one-to-one mapping is the property the lesson keeps
    using: boiling point tracks chain length, which is why a refinery tower can sort crude
    oil by chain length at all. The curve is schematic; the family trend it stands for is
    not.
  </p>

  <p>
    Methane (CH₄) is a gas; octane (C₈H₁₈) a volatile liquid; C₂₀+ chains are waxy solids. Same
    bonds, one trend: longer chains have more surface area to stick together with, so more
    energy (heat) is needed to boil them. This single trend is why an oil refinery's distillation
    tower works — crude oil sorts itself by chain length as it rises through cooling trays.
  </p>

  <h2>Why carbon, of all elements</h2>
  <p>
    Carbon's monopoly has three pillars. It is <strong>tetravalent</strong> — four bonds, the
    sweet spot between connectivity and stability. It <strong>catenates</strong>: C–C bonds
    (348 kJ/mol) are strong enough that long chains and rings survive ordinary conditions,
    while silicon's Si–Si bonds (226 kJ/mol) are too fragile to build a chemistry on. And it
    bonds <em>strongly</em> to hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen, so carbon frameworks come with
    chemically useful decorations attached. The result: with a handful of elements, an
    astronomically large combinatorial space — the counting chapter's geometric growth applied
    to molecules. Water hosts the chemistry; carbon builds the machinery.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> carbon builds chemistry because it bonds four ways, its chains
    survive and it decorates well — a combinatorial space bigger than every other element's
    combined. Navigating that space needs a language, and the naming system is it.
  </p>

  <h2>Formulae and names: the language of the subject</h2>
  <p>
    Organic compounds are written at four levels of detail. The <strong>molecular
    formula</strong> counts atoms (C₄H₁₀); the <strong>displayed/structural formula</strong>
    shows every bond; the <strong>empirical formula</strong> gives the simplest whole-number
    ratio (C₂H₅); and the <strong>skeletal formula</strong> — lines and vertices, heteroatoms
    labelled, carbons implied — is the professional shorthand you will see in every
    pharmacology paper. <strong>Naming</strong> follows IUPAC rules that reward practice over
    memorisation: count the longest chain for the parent (meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-,
    hex-, hept-, oct-…), choose the suffix for the family (-ane alkane, -ene alkene, -anol
    alcohol, -anoic acid), number from the end that gives substituents the lowest positions,
    and name branches alphabetically. Worked example: the five-carbon chain CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–
    CH₃ is 2-methylbutane — a butane parent (longest chain: 4), one methyl branch, numbered
    from the nearer end so the branch gets position 2. Get fluent here and every subsequent
    organic chapter is readable on sight.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vocabulary word that organises everything is the <strong>functional group</strong> —
    the atom or bond that does the chemistry. Alkanes have none (that is why they are inert);
    alkenes carry C=C; alcohols –OH; carboxylic acids –COOH; esters –COO–; halogenoalkanes
    –Cl/–Br. Families are defined by their group, so reactivity can be generalised across
    millions of compounds at once: learn one alkene reaction and you know ten thousand
    molecules' behaviour. (Alcohols, carboxylic acids and polymers get their own chapters;
    this page is the scaffolding they stand on.)
  </p>

  <h2>Alkanes: the inert workhorses</h2>
  <p>
    Alkanes are the <strong>saturated</strong> hydrocarbons — every carbon bonded to four
    neighbours, formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, tetrahedral geometry, free rotation about every C–C bond.
    Their defining property is the <strong>absence of reactivity</strong>: strong, nonpolar
    C–C and C–H bonds, no electron-poor sites, nothing for ionic reagents to grip. They do
    two things. They <strong>burn</strong> — complete combustion (CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O,
    ΔH = −890 kJ/mol) releases the energy that powers kitchens, turbines and engines;
    incomplete combustion, starved of oxygen, produces carbon monoxide (CO — the silent
    poison that binds haemoglobin 200× tighter than O₂) and soot, which is why gas heaters
    need ventilation. And they <strong>substitute</strong>: under UV light, a halogen
    replaces a hydrogen step by step (CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl), via a radical chain
    mechanism — a reaction used industrially and famously catastrophic when radicals meet
    ozone (the CFC story in the kinetics chapter).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> alkanes are the inert family — saturated, unreactive, doing
    only two things: burning and, under UV, substituting. Civilisation's fuel supply is
    mostly this family, which is why the refinery comes next.
  </p>

  <h2>Fuels: crude oil, fractions and cracking</h2>
  <p>
    Crude oil is ancient marine plankton, cooked and squeezed into a mixture of thousands of
    hydrocarbons. A refinery's first act is <strong>fractional distillation</strong>: heat the
    crude to about 400 °C, admit it to a tower that cools upward, and each fraction condenses
    at its band — refinery gases (C₁–C₄, bottled as LPG), petrol (C₄–C₈, 40–100 °C),
    naphtha (the chemical feedstock), kerosene (jet fuel, 150–240 °C), diesel and fuel oil,
    bitumen (road tar, over 350 °C). The tower is the boiling-point trend from the widget,
    run as an industry. But the market rarely matches the mixture: crude yields more heavy
    long chains than civilisation wants and less petrol than it buys — so the refinery
    <strong>cracks</strong> surplus heavy fractions: heat plus a zeolite catalyst snaps long
    chains into short alkanes <em>and</em> alkenes (C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄), simultaneously
    making petrol and the double-bond feedstock the plastics industry eats. Cracking is
    where refinery economics and organic synthesis shake hands; without it, petrol would be
    the by-product.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "organic chemistry" means natural, healthy or
  pesticide-free. In chemistry it means <em>carbon-containing</em> — a classification that
  includes petrol, methanol and nerve agents alongside honey and insulin. (The word's
  origin, from 19th-century vitalism — the belief organic molecules needed a living "vital
  force" — collapsed when Wöhler made urea, an animal-waste molecule, from ammonium cyanate
  in 1828.)</p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> benzene "has three double bonds alternating with three
  single ones". Every bond is the same — a hybrid halfway between, with the spare electrons
  spread around the whole ring. The alternating-bond picture survives only because Kekulé's
  hexagon got the geometry right, and textbooks still draw his symbol with a warning label.
  A related daily-life error: "all alkanes are gases." Only C₁–C₄ are; the same family
  spans cooking gas, octane and candle wax, one formula rule, one trend.</p>

  <h2>Alkenes: the double bond opens for business</h2>
  <p>
    One C=C double bond makes an alkene (CₙH₂ₙ) — <strong>unsaturated</strong> — and the
    second bond changes everything. The C=C unit is planar, electron-rich, and pinches open
    easily: alkenes are <strong>addition</strong> machines. Hydrogen across the double bond
    (with a nickel catalyst) hydrogenates oils into spreads; water adds across ethene to
    make ethanol (the industrial route to hand sanitiser's active ingredient); halogens add
    instantly — and <strong>bromine water, orange, decolourising on shaking, is the classic
    test for unsaturation</strong>. Most consequentially, alkenes <strong>polymerise</strong>:
    thousands of ethene molecules open their double bonds and link into poly(ethene), and
    the same trick builds PVC (from chloroethene), polystyrene and polypropylene — roughly
    400 million tonnes of plastic a year begin at an alkene double bond. The reactivity
    contrast with alkanes is the subject's cleanest before/after: one pair of electrons,
    four new industries.
  </p>

  <h2>Aromatics: the ring that rewrote the rules</h2>
  <p>
    Benzene (C₆H₆) should, by its formula, be desperate to react. Instead it is famously
    <em>reluctant</em> — and the puzzle took a century to solve. Kekulé's 1865 vision of a
    ring with alternating double and single bonds (allegedly from a dream of a snake biting
    its tail) explained the shape but not the stability: real benzene's six C–C bonds are
    all identical (139 pm, between C–C's 154 and C=C's 134), and its hydrogenation energy
    (−208 kJ/mol) is about 150 kJ/mol <em>less</em> exothermic than three separate double
    bonds would predict. The modern answer: the six extra electrons delocalise around the
    ring in a continuous π cloud — <strong>aromaticity</strong> — and the delocalised
    arrangement is lower in energy than any alternating-bond structure, so drawing benzene
    with fixed double bonds is simply wrong; the circle-in-hexagon is the honest picture.
    Aromatic rings (benzene, toluene, naphthalene) saturate the petrochemical and
    pharmaceutical worlds — aspirin, paracetamol and most dyes carry one — while their
    octane-boosting role in petrol and benzene's carcinogenicity (its metabolites wreck
    bone marrow) show the family's double-edged industrial character. Funnel-shaped
    real-world shorthand: alkanes burn, alkenes build, aromatics decorate and drug.
  </p>

  <h2>Isomers: same formula, different molecule</h2>
  <p>
    A formula does not fix a molecule. <strong>Structural isomers</strong> differ in how
    atoms connect: C₄H₁₀ is butane (straight, bp −0.5 °C) or isobutane (branched, bp
    −11.7 °C) — different compounds with different boiling points, densities and combustion
    behaviour. The count explodes with size (C₂₀H₄₂ has 366,319 structural isomers), which
    is why naming rules exist and why structure — not just composition — decides behaviour.
    <strong>Stereoisomers</strong> keep the connections identical and differ in geometry:
    but-2-ene comes in a <em>cis</em> form (both methyls on one side) and a <em>trans</em>
    form (opposite sides), with slightly different boiling points and very different
    biology — cis/trans geometry around C=C controls the shape of fats and the retinal
    molecule your retina flips to turn light into nerve signals. Isomerism is organic
    chemistry's first great lesson: molecules are shapes, and shapes have consequences.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "same formula, same behaviour." Isomerism is the standing
  counter-example: butane boils at −0.5 °C while its branched isomer isobutane boils eleven
  degrees colder, and cis- and trans-but-2-ene differ in the shape — and therefore the
  biology — of fats and retinal. Composition does not fix a molecule; structure does.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why are alkanes so unreactive, and which two reactions do they still do?</li>
    <li>Orange bromine water is shaken with a colourless liquid and loses its colour. What does that tell you, and what reaction happened?</li>
    <li>Crude oil yields more long chains than the market wants. What does the refinery do about it, and what two things does cracking gain?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Strong, nonpolar C–C and C–H bonds with no electron-poor
    sites give ionic reagents nothing to grip; the family burns (combustion) and, under UV
    light, substitutes halogens via radical chains. (2) The liquid was unsaturated — an
    alkene's C=C adds bromine across the double bond, the classic test for unsaturation.
    (3) It cracks surplus heavy fractions with heat plus a zeolite catalyst, gaining shorter
    alkanes for the petrol pool and alkenes as the plastics industry's feedstock.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Organic means carbon-containing — a statement about structure, not about being natural or healthy.</li>
    <li>Carbon's monopoly rests on three pillars: tetravalence, catenation, strong bonds to hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen.</li>
    <li>Longer chains boil higher — more surface contact — and the refinery exploits the trend twice: distillation sorts, cracking converts.</li>
    <li>Alkanes are inert workhorses: complete combustion gives carbon dioxide and water; starved of oxygen they make carbon monoxide and soot.</li>
    <li>Alkenes are addition machines: hydrogenation, hydration and polymerisation all open the C=C bond.</li>
    <li>Benzene's six C–C bonds are identical — aromaticity, not alternating double bonds.</li>
    <li>Isomers share a formula but not properties; structure, not composition, decides behaviour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The hydrocarbon families are the stage; the sibling intermediate lessons deepen the
    machinery: <strong>reaction kinetics</strong> explains why the double bond reacts so
    readily while the alkane waits, and <strong>redox and electrochemistry</strong>
    formalises the combustion and oxidation chemistry introduced here. The functional-group
    chapters then bolt –OH and –COOH onto the frameworks you can now name.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Octane rating: chemistry as knock prevention.</strong> Petrol engines detonate
    fuel prematurely ("knock") when compression is high. Straight-chain alkanes knock
    easily; branched ones resist. The octane rating is defined against two reference
    fuels — isooctane (2,2,4-trimethylpentane, rated 100) and n-heptane (rated 0) — so
    "95-octane" petrol resists knocking like a 95% isooctane blend. Refineries raise the
    rating by isomerisation, catalytic reforming (making aromatics) and additives — a
    century of tweaking what the cracking step made. The same chemistry explains why
    branched fuels, not long straight ones, fill the premium pump.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The saturation test in the kitchen and the lab.</strong> Bromine water's
    decolourising is the qualitative test; iodine value (grams of iodine absorbed per 100 g
    of fat) is the quantitative one, and it is why "polyunsaturated" appears on food labels
    — more C=C, more iodine consumed, runnier fat. Margarine manufacture is controlled
    hydrogenation: add just enough H₂ across the double bonds to stiffen the oil, with the
    unlucky side-effect of also making trans fats (why the process is now largely
    abandoned). One reaction, three industries: spreads, petrochemicals, analytical
    chemistry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Carbon beyond hydrocarbons.</strong> The scaffold keeps growing: fullerenes
    (C₆₀, a football of carbons, 1985), carbon nanotubes and graphene (one-atom-thick
    honeycomb, Nobel 2010) extended "carbon's shapes" from molecules to materials, and each
    carries a π-electron system as delocalised as benzene's — aromaticity scaled up. The
    registry of known carbon compounds passed 200 million entries in the 2020s; every one
    of them is reachable, in principle, by the naming rules and reaction patterns in these
    pages — which is the quiet argument for learning the vocabulary rather than the list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the tier goes next.</strong> Hydrocarbons are the stage; the functional
    groups are the actors. The next chapters add –OH (alcohols: solvents, biofuels, the
    ethanol story), oxidise it to –COOH (carboxylic acids: vinegar, aspirin, soaps via
    esters), and assemble small monomers into polymers at scale. Each new group is a new
    reactive site bolted onto the frameworks you now can name — and every one of them is
    found, unchanged, in the molecules of life.
  </p>
